The discipline of supramolecular chiral induction has expanded its reach as various chiral dopants were adopted for the onset of chirality in a wide range of supramolecular assemblies.[1] The chiral supramolecular assemblies resulting from chiral induction have so far been observed mainly for polymers[2,3,4] and oligomers (including foldamers[5]). Albeit several literatures[6] on chiral porous frameworks developed from chiral building blocks, induced chiral porous frameworks[7] came on the scene recently. Induced chiral SIMOF-1 as reported by Morris et al. was realized using chiral ionic liquids with template effect.[8] However the difficulties in removing the template from chiral pores reduce the accessibility of the pores. Bu et al. reported the synthesis of induced chiral MOF, namely, ATF-1P/M from achiral precursors using $(-)$ -cinchonidine/ $\left(+\right)$ -cinchonine as chiral catalysts.[9] The chiral induction concept has also been applied by Bu’s lab to convert zeolite-type zincophosphate into enantioenriched crystals.[10] The sergeants and soldiers experiment was adapted by Bu et al. to realize another induced chiral MOF, $(+)/(-)$ - $\mathsf{\Gamma}[\mathsf{M n}_{3}(\mathsf{H C O O})_{4}(\mathsf{a d c})]$ .[11] However, the sergeant D/L/DL-camphoric acid was not shown as recoverable in this chiral induction approach. Zaworotko et al.[12] recently adopted the solvent induction approach to obtain chiral MOF-5 using a achiral solvent, N-methyl-2-pyrrolidone; however, this is inconceivable with other solvents.[13] Till date, there is no paper to provide any evidence for the ee value of induced chiral MOFs. The present contribution imparts the efficiency of chiral induction for the first time in MOFs. It is important to mention that it is a common problem to prove that induced MOF is homochiral. Single-crystal X-ray diffraction and circular dichroism (CD) spectral analyses do not necessarily prove that homochiral MOF has been achieved after chiral induction. In this study, we have substantiated the induced chiral MOFs as chirality-enriched MOFs (CE-MOFs).
This study explores a strategic approach to derive CE-MOFs from racemic MOFs exemplified by $\mathsf{E u}(\mathsf{B T C})(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O})$ ·DMF, a highly stable, porous MOF that is the isostructural analog of MOF- $76^{[14]}$ and $N/\lfloor-78^{\lceil15\rceil}$ . $\mathsf{E u}(\mathsf{B T C})(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O})$ ·DMF crystallizes as a mixture in the tetragonal chiral space groups $P4_{3}22/P4_{1}22$ . Two decades old, MOF chemistry has advanced appreciably with MOFs finding a wide scope of applications thanks to its extended structures with ultrahigh surface areas and porosities.[16] Since $P4_{3}22$ and $P4_{1}22$ constitute an enantiomorphic space group pair, the $\mathsf{E u}(\mathsf{B T C})(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O})$ ·DMF framework would accommodate both $P4_{3}22$ and $P4_{1}22$ space groups thereby resulting in the framework optically inactive. In this regard, we interpret our study in connection with chiral unit cells (Scheme 1) characterized as $P$ ( $P$ implies plus) and M (M implies minus), conceding that the unit cells embody frameworks with right-and left-handed helices respectively. This work reports the synthesis of induced chiral MOFs with right-and left-handed helices in unequal proportions i.e. $P{\neq}M,$ different from the routine approaches[7] employed for the synthesis of induced chiral MOFs with right-and left-handed helices in equal proportions i.e. $\scriptstyle P=M.$ Of note, the CE-MOFs have the same frameworks as $\mathsf{E u}(\mathsf{B T C})(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O})$ ·DMF (Figures S1, S2) except for the enriched differences in handedness. The coordination environment and pore structure of the $P$ enriched MOF and the chiral channels of the CE-MOFs are illustrated in Figure 1.
FTIR analyses (Figure S1b) and crystal structures of the CEMOFs show no traces of chiral dopants in the pores of the washed CE-MOFs. The chiral dopants can be fully recovered by filtration followed by washing. Hence, the chiral dopants have most likely not acted as template in the  chiral induction mechanism. Had these acted as templates, a lot of amines would have been identified from the FTIR spectra of the CEMOFs. Hence we submit the following two routes (Scheme S1) as the preferential mechanism of chiral induction over template effect: First, 1,3,5-Benzenetricarboxylic acid, ${\sf H}_{3}{\sf B}{\sf T}{\sf C}$ , combines with the chiral amino alcohols (chiral dopants) by hydrogen bonds to form chiral building blocks, CBTC. Metal atoms then attack the chiral building blocks affording chirality enriched entities. Alternatively, the chiral dopant is coordinating to the metal which then combines with $\mathsf{H}_{3}\mathsf{B}\mathsf{T C}$ to give chirality enriched entities. 2-amino-1-butanol is a carefully designed chiral dopant where the $-O mathsf{H}$ group enables solubility in solvent mixture and the amino group develops strong H-bonding with chiral ligands to form MOFs. Importantly, we can use this dopant to synthesize $\mathsf{E u}(\mathsf{B T C})(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O})$ ·DMF. Besides, we have varied the amount of chiral dopant to tune the chirality of the MOFs (Figure S7) and to optimize the reaction condition for the chiral induced synthesis of
Scheme 1. Illustration of this study with reference to chiral unit cell. b, Incorporation of chiral dopants afforded the CE-MOFs. Eu, O, N, C and H atoms are colored cyan, red, blue, gray, and white, respectively. H atoms are excluded from framework and helix representations for clarity.
MOF. We found that the chiral signal improves upon increasing the amount of dopant from 0.1 to 10 molar equivalents. However there is almost no change in induced chirality from 10 to 100 molar equivalents. But 100 molar equivalents requires a large amount of chiral dopants which implies low efficiency. Hence 10 molar equivalents represents the optimized amount of chiral dopant.
To establish the chirality enrichment of the P-/M-variants, a batch of ten crystals was randomly selected out of which seven were $P.$ -enriched and three were M-enriched. The ee value thereby obtained for the CE-MOFs was $40\%$ . Importantly, the CE-MOFs in our study has an ee of $40\%$ and that it is not a homochiral MOF is not a scientific shortcoming. In fact, some common questions that may arise are: (i) Does addition of chiral dopant necessarily mean that homochiral MOF has been achieved? (ii) If one cannot get homochiral MOF, is the mixture (CE-MOFs) useful? (iii) Is the homochiral MOF the only choice and the mixture not useful? The present study addresses these questions with due consideration. As the next step, the chirality of the CE-MOFs was validated by CD spectroscopy that involved accessing the differential absorption between left- and right-circularly polarized UV radiation over the wavelength range 200-600 nm. CD spectroscopy necessitates CE-MOF particles to be smaller in size $(\sim1-5\mu\mathsf{m})$ because large crystals tend to show apparent CD signals. Shown in Figure 2a are the typical CD and UV-Vis absorption spectra of the CE-MOFs. The CEMOFs demonstrated intense induced CDs that are mirror images. The M-enriched MOF featuring a positive Cotton effect exhibits a peak at 252 nm, whereas the $P.$ enriched MOF featuring a negative Cotton effect exhibits a valley at 261 nm. The Cotton effects indicate the absolute configuration and bulkiness of the CE-MOFs. The $P-$ and M-enriched MOFs having opposite absolute configurations to each other showed opposite Cotton effect signs. In the wavelength range of 200-600 nm, the chiral dopants have no signal. The signal therefore comes from benzene ring of the chiral channels in the CE-MOFs.
With the aim for chiral recognition, the interaction between the pores (binding sites) of the CE-MOFs (host) and different chiral molecules (guest) was then studied.  Table 1 summarizes the binding affinities of CE-MOFs with chiral alcohols in different solvents. As shown in the first row of Table 1, control experiment was carried out with $\mathsf{E u}(\mathsf{B T C})(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O})$ ·DMF to confirm if it has the same chiral recognition capability to guest enantiomers, which would support the validity of the chiral recognition results.
Three solvents, namely, $\mathsf{C C l}_{4}$ , ${\mathsf{C H}}_{2}{\mathsf{C l}}_{2}$ and $C H_{3}C H_{2}O H$ , with diverse polarities were used to analyze the impact that solvent polarity would have on the binding affinity of the chiral alcohols to the host frameworks. It was found that chirality could be well recognized in solvents with lower polarities compared to those with higher polarities.  To illustrate the formation of host-guest complex with $P.$ enriched MOF as the host and (-)-2-Amino-1- butanol as the guest, the value of ${\cal K}_{\mathfrak{t}}$ diminishes from $\mathsf{9.3~}\mathsf{M}^{-1}$ in $C C l_{4}$ (polarity 1.6) to $5.2~\mathsf{M}^{-1}$ in $C H_{2}C l_{2}$ (polarity 3.4) and drops to
