$1.7~\mathsf{M}^{-1}$ in $C H_{3}C H_{2}O H$ (polarity 4.3). Notably, the ${\cal K}_{\mathfrak{t}}$ values in ethanol solvent are consistently low $(0.6-1.7~\mathsf{M}^{-1})$ irrespective of the host and guest. Ethanol being a solvent of high polarity is easy to be absorbed inside the chiral pores of the $P.$ and $M-$ variants for the chiral pores of the CE-MOFs are polarized. Hence no chiral recognition can be observed implying that there is no space for guest chiral molecules. The relationship between solvent polarity and chiral recognition ability of the hosts to chiral guest molecules is referred to as solvent effect. Shown in Figure 2b is the histogram of the ${\cal K}_{\mathfrak{t}}$ of $\mathsf{E u}(\mathsf{B T C})(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O})$ ·DMF and its $P/M$ variants with $\left(+\right)$ -2-Amino-1-butanol and (-)-2-Amino-1-butanol in $C C\vert_{4}$ , $C H_{2}C l_{2}$ , and $C H_{3}C H_{2}O H$ solvents.
The amino group can considerably bind with metal. With the intent to ascertain the chiral recognition ability of the CE-MOFs for guests with other groups, a guest of same molecular size as 2-Amino-3-methyl-1-butanol but different molecular species, namely, (-)-2-methyl-2,4-pentanediol, was used. However, the CE-MOFs accommodated minimal binding site for the species. The ${\mathsf{K}}_{\mathtt{f}}$ value considering the formation of host-guest complex with $P.$ -variant as host and $(-)$ -2-Amino-3-methyl-1-butanol as guest in ${\mathsf{C H}}_{2}{\mathsf{C l}}_{2}$ solvent is $3.1~\mathsf{M}_{4}^{-1}$ , whereas the value decreases to $0.8\ \mathsf{M}^{-1}$ for (-)-2-methyl-2,4-pentanediol as guest with other factors remaining unchanged. This effect is called coordination effect. Figure 2d depicts the ${\cal K}_{\mathfrak{t}}$ of the $\mathsf{\Gamma}P.$ variant with (-)-2-Amino3-methyl-1-butanol and $(-)$ -2-methyl-2,4-pentanediol  in $C C l_{4}$ solvent.
To explore the functional aspects of the CE-MOFs to greater heights, MMMs[17] comprising the CE-MOF particles as filler were fabricated. PIM-1, a linear polymer incorporating $-C=N$ groups that reinforce intermolecular interactions and increase the free volume, was adopted as the continuous matrix in this study.[18] Notably, several polymer/filler weight ratios had been tried out; however, $30~\mathrm{wt\%}$ CE-MOF particles when included into the PIM-1 matrix gave the best results. MMMs with less than 30 $w t\%$ filler proved unproductive for chiral separation, whereas filler concentration higher than $30\mathrm{\ut\%}$ hampered the fabrication of MMMs. SEM images of the MMMs revealed CE-MOF particles embedded in the PIM-1 matrix (Figure 3). The crystalline CE-MOF particles contribute to the XRD peaks (Figure S61) of the MMMs. The enantiopurity of 2-amino-1- butanol, a chiral liquid, was evaluated in terms of $6e\%$ from the optical rotation of the sample measured with a polarimeter. As shown in Table S2, the ee values obtained from the permeation tests of the membranes are about $9\%$ when n-hexane (polarity index 0.1) was used as the solvent in the feed solution. However, the ee values decrease when solvents of higher polarities e.g. water (polarity index 10.2) and ethanol (polarity index 4.3) are used. Importantly, the permeation results of the membranes are in keeping with the chiral recognition results by the CE-MOFs. The MMMs show better enantioselective permeation performance in solvents with lower polarities compared to those with higher polarities. Of note, the ee value assessed for the CEMOFs was about $40\%$ . In other words, no enantiopure framework was adopted in this study. Nevertheless, the $6e\%$ value for enantioselective permeation of chiral analytes through the MMMs is not limited and can be improved that is merely a technical aspect and currently underway in our lab. It is important to carry out CD analysis of the CE-MOFs before and after the chiral separation experiment. We found that even though CD signal decreases after chiral separation, it still retains chirality (Figure S63). As the polarity of solvent in feed solution increases, the stability of MOFs decreases. For n-hexane, there was almost no change in the CD signal before and after chiral separation. CD signal decreases slightly for ethanol and water.
In light of the facts and findings that PIM-1 and the CEMOFs are inexpensive, the chiral dopants are fully recoverable and that solvent polarity plays an important role in the chiral recognition and separation of the CE-MOFs, this study is useful and can be extended to other MOFs crystallized in chiral space groups and may bring to light pioneering materials applications.
